1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,255
Welcome to Class 10: R notebooks and large-data analysis.

2
00:00:04,255 --> 00:00:08,509
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:15,128
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:15,128 --> 00:00:17,491
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17,491 --> 00:00:22,689
After this class, you can: run R in a controlled Conda environment;

6
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:28,319
use an R kernel in JupyterLab when needed; analyse larger tables with data.table,

7
00:00:28,319 --> 00:00:34,816
DuckDB, Arrow, and dplyr patterns; build figures with ggplot2; separate exploratory notebooks from batch analysis;

8
00:00:34,816 --> 00:00:39,147
prepare R code that can be rerun by a colleague.

9
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:45,815
Start from the provided example: For an interactive R notebook, use the Jupyter example from Class

10
00:00:45,815 --> 00:00:46,231
9.

11
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:49,148
The environment already includes an R kernel.

12
00:00:49,148 --> 00:00:53,230
Do not begin by loading every file into memory.

13
00:00:53,230 --> 00:01:00,033
First check: file size; number of columns; expected row count; whether the file is compressed;

14
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:06,382
whether the analysis needs all columns; whether the operation is streaming, grouped, or random-access.

15
00:01:06,382 --> 00:01:07,742
Good starting points:

16
00:01:07,742 --> 00:01:13,926
For teaching and small examples, the course uses Conda environments because they are easy to reproduce

17
00:01:13,926 --> 00:01:15,085
in Slurm jobs.

18
00:01:15,085 --> 00:01:21,268
For larger R projects, you may also use renv, but do not keep active package libraries

19
00:01:21,268 --> 00:01:24,746
with many small files on shared storage while computing.

20
00:01:24,746 --> 00:01:28,997
Restore packages into local job storage or use a reviewed container.

21
00:01:28,997 --> 00:01:43,010
The course includes: examples/interactive-workflows/notebooks/r-large-data.ipynb examples/interactive-workflows/r/analysis.R examples/interactive-workflows/r/r.sbatch examples/interactive-workflows/r/environment.yml The notebook demonstrates group summaries and a sampled figure.

22
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:51,769
The batch example writes reproducible output into a results directory.

23
00:01:51,769 --> 00:01:55,995
Request only the CPU and memory your script can use.

24
00:01:55,995 --> 00:01:59,377
Keep raw data compressed where tools support it.

25
00:01:59,377 --> 00:02:03,181
Avoid thousands of tiny temporary files on shared storage.

26
00:02:03,181 --> 00:02:05,717
Use node-local scratch for intermediate output.

27
00:02:05,717 --> 00:02:09,944
Save final tables and figures back to the project area.

28
00:02:09,944 --> 00:02:14,171
Use sacct after completion to compare requested and observed memory.

29
00:02:14,171 --> 00:02:19,346
Run: Then submit the R batch job once and verify that the result file was written.

30
00:02:19,346 --> 00:02:24,521
The gate is complete when you can explain how the same code could be moved from

31
00:02:24,521 --> 00:02:26,462
notebook exploration into the batch script.

